#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055014 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ad705d6-c77f-5a47-9a1e-5798a2e69b5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}, 0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051615} {:answer/id #uuid "dfcedc71-cf7d-5b65-af9e-54428e26e058" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{2}}, 0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051616} {:answer/id #uuid "e0009580-02f5-52dd-98f1-b07dbfc99cf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}}, 0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051617} {:answer/id #uuid "33551d7d-90e1-5c07-b924-3ac69df5265e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{2}}, 0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051618} {:answer/id #uuid "3ccc5722-bf35-575a-8a09-a915ad74fff4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{4}{5\\sqrt{2}}, 0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0009580-02f5-52dd-98f1-b07dbfc99cf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}}, 0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051617} :answer-field/id #uuid "10c0ed9f-9e4a-5ad5-ae27-d4b346d556e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051614}] :question/id #uuid "53178a84-d201-5a6c-9c41-fb88e803ebbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-15140" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aab03da9e57863ce49011b6e6d9e1cd2cb64aa4c3eabfe5f323f505074c3b987.png)\n\nThe equation $x^{2} + 4y^{2} = 6$ represents an ellipse. The normal line at the point $(\\sqrt{2}, 1)$ on the ellipse intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P$. What are the coordinates of $P$?" :question/worked-solution "Implicit differentiation gives the tangent slope $-x/(4y)=-1/(2\\sqrt2)$ at $(\\sqrt2,1)$. The normal slope is its negative reciprocal, $2\\sqrt2$. Thus the normal is $y-1=2\\sqrt2(x-\\sqrt2)$. Setting $y=0$ yields $$x=\\sqrt2-\\frac1{2\\sqrt2}=\\frac3{2\\sqrt2}.$$ Therefore $P=\\left(\\tfrac3{2\\sqrt2},0\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cae9c0a3-aa69-501e-9e6e-8e65972b3220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051633} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d87b76-34ca-5101-b173-4c4d2d6dd80c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(0, \\frac{14}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051634} {:answer/id #uuid "2a7a1216-d248-5790-8d4e-32c43f11b949" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051635} {:answer/id #uuid "33ddcc32-b317-5d4d-8276-63aa3cc2da82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(0, \\frac{10}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051636} {:answer/id #uuid "696ae730-4f99-5f63-85b6-0d612888f8a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(0, \\frac{5}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33ddcc32-b317-5d4d-8276-63aa3cc2da82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(0, \\frac{10}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051636} :answer-field/id #uuid "5066968b-9998-5c60-9663-8898df06cb26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051632}] :question/id #uuid "3f326a76-bac1-526b-a118-b84853c87e79" :question/math-academy-id "q-15259" :question/problem "![](images/4a/4a5fd15c2282e11fda4e98f64557d0b69d79bbcdc26946a3f494841233c680be.png)\n\nThe equation $3x^{2} - 2y^{2} = 4$ represents a hyperbola. The normal line at the point $(2, 2)$ on the hyperbola intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P$. What are the coordinates of $P$?" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating gives $6x-4y\\,y'=0$, hence the tangent slope at $(2,2)$ is $3/2$. The normal slope is $-2/3$, so its equation is $y-2=-\\tfrac23(x-2)$. At $x=0$, $y=2+\\tfrac43=\\tfrac{10}3$. Thus $P=\\left(0,\\tfrac{10}3\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092597 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83908dd0-d198-52b3-8826-afa1429dbb50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089754} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae2cd5b-f109-5dbb-bf75-ab22f3567103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089755} {:answer/id #uuid "0c3da346-d7d9-5148-b044-6261a6a7a773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089756} {:answer/id #uuid "46f644a7-41c7-575e-90ea-b0bdf7907385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\frac{1}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089757} {:answer/id #uuid "4e49a3e0-69dd-5cc5-8e4e-d960cd9640b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ae2cd5b-f109-5dbb-bf75-ab22f3567103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089755} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c74ba52-6920-5ddb-99ba-e1cd12c20908" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089753}] :question/id #uuid "a5be0c71-ace5-571a-88cf-4ce8508ffc58" :question/math-academy-id "q-17133" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $1$, and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{5π}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P$?" :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$. With $r=1$ and $\\theta=5\\pi/3$, the coordinates are $\\left(\\tfrac12,-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a09efa26-c8e0-5412-ab88-3198ac483f85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(-\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}, 5\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091349} {:answer/id #uuid "b4663627-6e87-5b9e-ad2a-b45c4d9efc23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091350} {:answer/id #uuid "c31e9187-659c-536b-a14a-1ed24aef0f88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},-5\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091351} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc26997-f5ab-5e99-9de1-52c2d410ae79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(5, \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091352} {:answer/id #uuid "79adfbc5-24f9-5b5d-b471-2c462b1283f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79adfbc5-24f9-5b5d-b471-2c462b1283f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091353} :answer-field/id #uuid "3904dc85-2480-5f7c-8b2a-0941e3ba06f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091348}] :question/id #uuid "a131e90c-6bee-5a11-8f5e-31ad30812f77" :question/math-academy-id "q-8522" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $5$, and that $P$ makes an angle of $330^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P$?" :question/worked-solution "With $r=5$ and $\\theta=330^\\circ$, $$P=(5\\cos330^\\circ,5\\sin330^\\circ)=\\left(\\tfrac{5\\sqrt3}2,-\\tfrac52\\right).$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097161 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae96c78f-e10d-563b-82af-e2c02160928e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{29}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094940} {:answer/id #uuid "25f7a90e-939a-568d-ba3d-07a616fcb021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{q + r}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094941} {:answer/id #uuid "328e6ad2-ef2f-5827-8881-60cdfaebb76b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2q}{\\sqrt{13}} + \\frac{r}{\\sqrt{29}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094942} {:answer/id #uuid "681555fa-cdb3-5281-b943-0cf1eed80c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2(q + r))/(\\sqrt{73})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094943} {:answer/id #uuid "703e463a-8826-5adf-9d3d-36bfd8037d83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{q + r}{\\sqrt{13}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "681555fa-cdb3-5281-b943-0cf1eed80c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2(q + r))/(\\sqrt{73})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094943} :answer-field/id #uuid "96962338-e491-50f7-b8da-1efc8b69ba83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094939}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9d05ca0e-131b-5294-8367-1760396e4369" :question/math-academy-id "q-19276" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9dfaf74a497a2fd22428e09a4c26d573e184c9728ccf67f11ebb9d6a2a44f4c6.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $q + r$?" :question/worked-solution "The scale shows that two grid squares represent one unit. Reading the displacements from the diagram gives\n\n$$\nq=\\left\\langle\\frac52,-\\frac32\\right\\rangle,\n\\qquad r=\\left\\langle\\frac32,3\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThus $q+r=\\left\\langle4,\\frac32\\right\\rangle$ and\n\n$$\n|q+r|=\\sqrt{4^2+\\left(\\frac32\\right)^2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{73}}2.\n$$\n\nThe required unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\frac{q+r}{|q+r|}=\\frac{2(q+r)}{\\sqrt{73}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484536 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cff24c1e-e65f-5b03-b5f1-70eb31b0ce47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\ln28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cff24c1e-e65f-5b03-b5f1-70eb31b0ce47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\ln28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484738} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0676d28-765c-57d3-9eac-387eb874584e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484721}] :question/id #uuid "8d7812fe-a1d4-58bb-aa3c-1a08f58e56a4" :question/problem "Source question: Problem 4, PDF page 5.\n\nCalculate the work done by $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\dfrac{\\langle x,y,z\\rangle}{x^2+y^2+z^2}$ moving an object from $(1,1,1)$ to $(2,4,8)$. Show your work.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." :question/worked-solution "For nonzero position vectors, $\\mathbf F=\\nabla\\left[\\tfrac12\\ln(x^2+y^2+z^2)\\right]$. The fundamental theorem gives work $\\tfrac12\\ln84-\\tfrac12\\ln3=\\tfrac12\\ln28$. The field is undefined at the origin; any admissible path avoids it. No particular path is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672160 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f47087f8-1a0e-5ca3-bf46-0b8838b955b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\left(9\\ln x+\\frac12\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f47087f8-1a0e-5ca3-bf46-0b8838b955b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\left(9\\ln x+\\frac12\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672413} :answer-field/id #uuid "c926c7e0-ca74-5227-b6cf-cd8ca8c26af9" :answer-field/key "u" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672354}] :question/id #uuid "44a2e885-5683-5315-afa6-f62a1c697bd7" :question/problem "**(d)** Solve the linear equation from (b), using the initial condition from (c).\n\n$u(x)=$ {{u}}." :question/worked-solution "For x>0, the integrating factor for $u\\prime-u/x=9$ is 1/x. Then $(u/x)\\prime=9/x$, giving u/x=9ln x+C. The condition u(1)=1/2 gives C=1/2."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697162 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e61cb88-4009-5620-84fc-a9080a4da648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186697157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e61cb88-4009-5620-84fc-a9080a4da648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186697157} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c66ad95-0e1a-5f4f-b282-666f579aebe1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186697158}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9bc757c8-8f4c-5efa-890c-24f6558197c9" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x^2-3x+3}}$, complete the expression for $f'(x)$ by entering the missing factor.\n\n$f'(x)=\\dfrac{1}{(x^2-3x+3)^{3/2}}\\,($ {{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=x^2-3x+3$ and rewrite the outer function as $F(u)=2u^{-1/2}$, so $f(x)=F(g(x))$.\n\nDifferentiate the outer and inner functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF'(u)&=2\\left(-\\frac12\\right)u^{-3/2}=-u^{-3/2}, \\\\\ng'(x)&=2x-3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x)&=F'(g(x))g'(x) \\\\\n&=-(x^2-3x+3)^{-3/2}(2x-3) \\\\\n&=\\frac{3-2x}{(x^2-3x+3)^{3/2}} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{(x^2-3x+3)^{3/2}}(3-2x).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing factor is $3-2x$. It includes both the inner derivative and the outer derivative's factor of $-1$.\n\nSince $x^2-3x+3=\\left(x-\\frac32\\right)^2+\\frac34>0$, this derivative is defined for every real $x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "038335aa-260a-541e-9ea8-37be5be5df8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(x+\\frac32\\right)^2-\\frac72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186701572} {:answer/id #uuid "1c15ca70-e229-5a0a-847f-361e480cba02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(x+\\frac34\\right)^2-\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186701573} {:answer/id #uuid "2da1306c-2477-5e82-961b-1dc298481197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(x+\\frac32\\right)^2+\\frac14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186701574} {:answer/id #uuid "678ea45f-6852-5250-90d2-7c7f4c5ccdc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(x+\\frac32\\right)^2-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186701580} {:answer/id #uuid "8d0dcbc2-c3f9-5fd2-99e3-a09a2e9df046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(x+\\frac32\\right)^2-\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186701581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d0dcbc2-c3f9-5fd2-99e3-a09a2e9df046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(x+\\frac32\\right)^2-\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186701581} :answer-field/id #uuid "90dbd50a-2800-5333-98e8-447ae9e65932" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186701583}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6617564c-2d2f-5f34-b8cf-2806a97f2dfc" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2+3x-2$." :question/worked-solution "Rewrite the linear coefficient with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2+2\\cdot\\frac32 x-2.\n$$\n\nAdd and subtract $\\left(\\frac32\\right)^2$ so the expression keeps the same value:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2+2\\cdot\\frac32 x+\\left(\\frac32\\right)^2}-\\left(\\frac32\\right)^2-2.\n$$\n\nFactor the perfect square, then combine the constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(x+\\frac32\\right)^2-\\frac94-2\n&=\\left(x+\\frac32\\right)^2-\\frac94-\\frac84\\\\\n&=\\left(x+\\frac32\\right)^2-\\frac{17}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186741867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34ce61ee-7f34-5869-a3d7-69ee9d5f72a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x^2-2x-4}{(x^2+x+1)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186741864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34ce61ee-7f34-5869-a3d7-69ee9d5f72a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x^2-2x-4}{(x^2+x+1)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186741864} :answer-field/id #uuid "b59dfe40-9c4a-53be-adbe-7bd713479293" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186741868}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "84f14476-4272-5e71-8a8b-0b5f602a1564" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{x^2-2x+2}{x^2+x+1}$, find $f'(x).$\n\n$f'(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u(x)=x^2-2x+2$ and $v(x)=x^2+x+1$. Then\n\n$$\nu'(x)=2x-2,\\qquad v'(x)=2x+1.\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule gives\n\n$$\nf'(x)=\\frac{u'(x)v(x)-u(x)v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2}.\n$$\n\nExpand the two products in the numerator separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u'(x)v(x)&=(2x-2)(x^2+x+1)\\\\&=2x^3+2x^2+2x-2x^2-2x-2\\\\&=2x^3-2,\\\\u(x)v'(x)&=(x^2-2x+2)(2x+1)\\\\&=2x^3+x^2-4x^2-2x+4x+2\\\\&=2x^3-3x^2+2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract these products and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=\\frac{(2x^3-2)-(2x^3-3x^2+2x+2)}{(x^2+x+1)^2}\\\\&=\\frac{2x^3-2-2x^3+3x^2-2x-2}{(x^2+x+1)^2}\\\\&=\\frac{3x^2-2x-4}{(x^2+x+1)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cubic terms cancel. The derivative is valid for every real $x$, since $x^2+x+1=\\left(x+\\tfrac12\\right)^2+\\tfrac34>0$."}]}
